================================================================================
CROSS-CUTTING INVARIANT — identity/storage rule for anything CX-backed
================================================================================
The CX layer resolves a ContextId to a module's raw user_data pointer ONCE
and reuses that SAME pointer across many later calls, without re-resolving
it each time. So: a fixed-array/pointer-backed module must NEVER move a
surviving item's storage once a ContextId has been handed out for it - no
compaction on remove. Free slots become gaps/tombstones, reused only by a
later NEW item (which gets a new identity). Already how samples/plugins
work (contexts/sampler.c, app_data.c's ops tables), and how params.c's own
param_add_param/params_container_resync work (NULL-tombstone a freed
slot, never shift a survivor).

================================================================================
STAGE 3 — parameters as CX contexts, integrated into the action system
================================================================================
Agreed direction: params become CX tree nodes (like plugin instances),
not a live external list (like JACK ports). Build the full vertical
slice (steps below) on CLAP first, then replicate the same shape to
LV2/sampler/synth - each has its own small deltas, noted per step.

STEPS 0-4: DONE for CLAP. Verified via clean build (-Wall -Wextra, no
new warnings) and a real pty-driven CLI run loading SG-323 under
valgrind (0 definitely/indirectly/possibly lost; the only reported
errors are a pre-existing uninitialised-value warning inside SG-323's
own JUCE code, unrelated to this work) - params show up as real named
